  "band": "SOTA TRANSFERS (vs recipe-matched negatives)",
  "confound": "Every separating feature moves in the direction MORE TRAINING would also move it: larger sigma1 and Frobenius norm, higher energy concentration, lower spectral entropy, heavier tails. The organisms were trained on 60,237 synthetic conversations (source paper 4.1); the five recipe-matched benign adapters are small task fine-tunes of unknown but almost certainly much smaller scale. Salama et al., 'Dataset Size Recovery from LoRA Weights' (arXiv:2406.19395), state verbatim that 'both the norm and the spectrum of the LoRA matrices are closely linked to the fine-tuning dataset size' -- the SAME two quantities this detector uses. Recipe-matching is therefore NOT sufficient: without volume-matched negatives, a spectral backdoor detector and a dataset-size estimator are not distinguishable on these features.",
  "scope": "n=2 positives. We did not reproduce the published benchmark, did not measure the benign adapters' dataset sizes, and make no claim that their reported accuracy is wrong. The claim is about what a recipe-matched control can and cannot license."
}